 detail = """cognitive() walks one function's body by recursive descent carrying a nesting level (not ast.walk, which loses depth), adding 1 + nesting for each If/For/AsyncFor/While/ExceptHandler/IfExp/Match, 1 flat for a real else, a nesting increment with no base increment for a nested FunctionDef/AsyncFunctionDef/Lambda, one per RUN of like operators inside a BoolOp, and 1 for a recursive call. An elif chain is FLATTENED before descent: the parser nests it inside orelse, so a naive recursion both double-counts and over-nests every ladder. census() walks the declared source paths (excluding __pycache__), keying each function on (relative path, qualified name) so a module move is spelled rather than double-counted, and returns every source-function row plus a per-module public-symbol count that is emitted and never compared. main() selects only rows strictly over the threshold for baseline comparison, reads docs/complexity-baseline as tab-separated rows, compares by exact equality in both directions, prints the three sanctioned remedies on a finding, and honours no per-site suppression token from the scanned source; --report always exits 0, --restamp rewrites the baseline and compares nothing, --mode warn (the default) reports a divergence but still exits 0, and --mode enforce returns nonzero on a divergence in either direction. Standard library only. The shipped downstream template remains REPORT-ONLY: it wires no [step:complexity], so its enforce capability is exercised by this module's own tests until an adopter opts in. This repository has made that separate opt-in: its [step:complexity] in docs/stack.ini is layer=product at DevStg-Impl and runs --mode enforce over project-trajectory/scripts/ and tests/."""
 rationale = """The counting rules are pinned in this module's own docstring rather than delegated, because a census whose rules live in a third-party tool has a baseline that is a property of that tool's version: an upgrade that began counting a new construct would invalidate every entry at once, with no code having changed. Keying on (path, qualified name) rather than on the bare name is what lets a function move between modules as one re-keyed row instead of appearing twice. The absence of a per-site suppression token is stated here rather than left to the implementation because it is the clause most likely to be added later as a convenience: scattered opt-outs self-replicate as new code copies them, and a central file is the only form of this escape hatch that stays auditable in one read. Landing Drafted because its parent SR-183 is itself Drafted and approving this design is the owner's act."""
