+module = "project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py"
+code_symbol = "RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/round_count/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers"
+detail = """The reading behind the merge seam's verdict rung, sited below both of its readers. tree_identity folds `git ls-tree -r` with the RECORD prefixes dropped - docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/, docs/iteration/ - into a SHA-256 over the surviving entries, which is what makes "the tree a verdict judged" a value two independent readers can compare; the digest is 64 hex deliberately, so it cannot be mistaken for the git tree object id the Bar-Green trailer carries. fold_listing is pure over the listing, so the one rule that decides whether two trees are the same work is exercisable without a repository. logged_rounds is the provenance join: a file under docs/reviews/ is a ROUND only when the coordinator's own committed session log for that (train, ordinal) declares a REVIEW phase, so an implementer-authored file in the review path is not evidence. round_entries restricts the rounds to those whose reviewed commit carries the identity under judgement and hands the phase/ordinal/verdict triples to the latest-per-phase rule the scoring layer already owned; the parse function is INJECTED rather than imported, so this leaf keeps no edge back into scoring. round_count gives `rounds=<N>` its evidence-derived scope: completed review cycles represented at ONE governing tree, computed as the largest per-phase entry count because one cycle may carry REVIEW-A and REVIEW-B; changing the work changes the identity and starts this count over. branch_trailers reads the Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex> machine half off the branch's own commits, verified against the carrying commit's non-record tree exactly as the bar attestation is verified against its own. Off git every reader answers None and says nothing about what that means; the two callers declare opposite-looking fail directions that are the same direction - refuse the merge, assume a round is owed."""
+rationale = """The gate and the loop ask one question from two sides - may this merge, and does this lane still owe a round - so one definition below both readers prevents process-record commits or unlogged files from producing different answers. The trailer is deliberately NOT an accept path and the module says so: anyone with commit access can stamp one on their own commit and the self-verification will pass, because it is their tree - what a session cannot forge is the coordinator's committed session log, so the round evidence decides and the trailer is read as a cross-check that refuses when either its verdict word or its evidence-derived round count contradicts the rounds. Binding by identity rather than by ordering makes staleness the direct question "does this still describe the thing", with no last-one-wins rule that could promote an approval for different work."""
